# 14. DATA MIGRATION STRATEGY

This is the most critical part of the project.

Perform migration in the following order.

### Phase 1: Existing System Audit

Inspect all original source files and spreadsheet worksheets.

Produce:

- Existing module inventory.
- Existing feature inventory.
- Existing database structure.
- Existing column mapping.
- Existing user-role mapping.
- Existing workflow documentation.
- Existing calculation documentation.

### Phase 2: Data Backup

Create a complete backup of the original Google Sheets database.

Preserve all worksheets and original records.

Do not modify the original source.

### Phase 3: Data Mapping

Map every original spreadsheet column to its corresponding MySQL table and column.

Create a migration mapping document.

No source column should be silently ignored.

### Phase 4: Database Creation

Create the DIPEN_ERP database.

Implement all required tables and relationships.

### Phase 5: Test Migration

Import a copy of the original data.

Validate:

- Record counts.
- Unique IDs.
- Document numbers.
- Foreign key relationships.
- Opening stock.
- Closing stock.
- Purchase totals.
- BOM calculations.
- Production quantities.
- Dispatch quantities.
- Tender records.

### Phase 6: Functional Parity Testing

Compare the original ERP and new ERP using identical test cases.

Every important existing workflow must produce equivalent results.

### Phase 7: User Acceptance Testing

Allow authorized users to test the new ERP.

Record all errors and missing features.

Do not declare the migration complete until the identified critical issues are resolved.

### Phase 8: Production Cutover

Only after formal approval:

1. Create a final backup.
2. Temporarily stop new entries in the original ERP.
3. Migrate the latest records and transactions.
4. Validate record counts and balances.
5. Activate the new Java ERP.
6. Keep the old ERP available in read-only mode.
7. Monitor the new system.

### Phase 9: Rollback Plan

If a serious problem occurs:

- Stop new transactions in the new ERP.
- Preserve its transaction logs.
- Restore the last verified backup if required.
- Re-enable the original ERP.
- Reconcile transactions made after migration.
- Investigate and correct the issue.

Never delete the original application or original spreadsheet as part of deployment.

